  ],
  "title": "Gingilini\u0161",
  "provenance": "Pleiades",
  "placeTypeURIs": [
    "https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/place-types/settlement"
  ],
  "details": "<p>According to the \"Bavian Inscription\" of the Neo-Assyrian king Sennacherib, <span class=\"w\" style=\"text-align: start; \">Gingilini\u0161</span><span style=\"text-align: start; float: none; \"></span> was one of eighteen cities from which canals were dug to direct water to the main canal and aqueduct system that was being built to bring vast amounts of water to the Assyrian capital Nineveh.</p>\r\n<p>\u00a0</p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "A town mentioned in an Akkadian inscription of the Neo-Assyrian king Sennacherib. This place is generally identified with the modern Tepe Chenchi.",
  "reprPoint": [
